{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1548 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b9ac0e9f231ca19c00fdc1abd651d1a83f19f39f26db75a2210021cbef049230" :edb/db-before-t 1547 :edb/db-after-t 1548 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534860 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:12:38.598Z" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186094675 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094679 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441435 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441437 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441438 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441440 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441442 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441436 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441439 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441441 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186096904 1050 720.0 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186097117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186097117 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-9,5)$ and passes through the point $(7,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-9,5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}=4p(x+9).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(7,1),$ we substitute $x=7$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-5)^{2} & =4p(7+9) \\\\ 16 & =4p(16) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-5)^{2} & =(x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-5)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =5\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$\nand $8.$" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186097868 1064 "Let's consider the parabola $(x-1)^2 = \\dfrac 1 2 (y+3) ,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bbe360c9580cab435e5b737c80c5381f039d1871250cdae3c16bc686e3d0a53d.png)\n\n  \n\nTo calculate the $y$-intercept of the parabola (shown as the point $A$ in the diagram), we set $x=0$ and solve for $y.$ In our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (0-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 2 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =2-3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-1).$\n\nOn the other hand, to find the $x$-intercepts (shown as the points $B$ and $C$ in the diagram), we set $y=0$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right)$ and the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1+\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right).$" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186097868 1064 "Let's consider the parabola $(x-1)^2 = \\dfrac 1 2 (y+3) ,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bbe360c9580cab435e5b737c80c5381f039d1871250cdae3c16bc686e3d0a53d.png)\n\nTo calculate the $y$-intercept of the parabola (shown as the point $A$ in the diagram), we set $x=0$ and solve for $y.$ In our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (0-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 2 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =2-3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-1).$\n\nOn the other hand, to find the $x$-intercepts (shown as the points $B$ and $C$ in the diagram), we set $y=0$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right)$ and the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1+\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right).$" 13194139534860 17592186045417 false] [17592186441391 1080 "0" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441391 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441391 1083 #uuid "0153ff6d-d05a-56ce-9b58-02c01757d0ac" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441392 1080 "$(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-8\\sqrt{2})$" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441392 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441392 1083 #uuid "05b4bc6f-6eae-58f3-bd4e-a6df1a6e19f7" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441393 1080 "-2" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441393 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441393 1083 #uuid "15ab6d36-955b-5319-b4c2-4ceabe2971b7" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441394 1080 "(-27,0)" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441394 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441394 1083 #uuid "161002e7-3475-55d1-bf33-13e031fadd4a" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441395 1080 "6" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441395 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441395 1083 #uuid "199f0fce-6ad5-5b52-bb36-a5c2e347b81d" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441396 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [17592186441396 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534860 17592186045417 true] [175921864413